think turn carries the networking key and two counters that only ever climb — key_counter on ordinary rotation, reset_counter on reset; the identity key signs net_pubkey ++ both counters, so a rotation is authentic and a bumped counter breaks the signature
think a capability is one power (tend management · sow spawn · hand transfer) over one point in one holder's hand; the keeper signs point ++ power ++ holder, so the holder's claim traces to the keeper and a swapped power refuses; a capability moves by a fresh signed grant, never a copy
think sponsor records the arithmetic default parent and a chosen parent; the escape is the child's word alone, so the child's identity key signs point ++ chosen_parent
think signed messages use little-endian integer bytes (std.mem.writeInt) so a signature is the same on every target; message builders copy the pubkey with a bounded loop rather than a banned @memcpy
